1. Sales trend
Finding: Monthly sales peaked in March at about ₹1.93M, then fell steadily to about ₹1.62M by December, roughly 16% lower. Orders followed the same pattern, from about 2,830 in March to about 2,380 in December.
Why it matters: Sales dropped in the second half of the year and didn't recover, even in the months before the festive season.
Recommendation: Plan promotions for the slow months (September to December) and find out why demand fell.

2. Customer mix
Finding: Women place 69.42% of orders. Adult women alone make up 52.39% of all orders, and adults of both genders are about 76% of orders. Teenagers account for under 5%.
Why it matters: The business depends on adult women.
Recommendation: Focus marketing and new product ranges on this group, and test whether men and teenagers are an untapped segment.

3. Sales channels
Finding: Amazon brings in the most (₹7.52M, about 35% of sales), followed by Myntra (₹4.94M) and Flipkart (₹4.57M). Together these three bring in about 80% of sales. Meesho, Nalli and Others each bring in about ₹1M or less.
Why it matters: Sales depend heavily on three marketplaces, so a policy or fee change on Amazon would hurt a lot.
Recommendation: Keep investing in the top three, and review whether the small channels are worth the effort or need a new approach.

4. Order outcomes
Finding: 92.25% of orders were delivered (28,641). The other 7.75% (2,406 orders) were returned (1,045), cancelled (844) or refunded (517).
Why it matters: If those orders are included in Total Sales, real revenue is lower than ₹2.12 Cr. At the average order value, about ₹16 lakh of sales didn't turn into delivered revenue. That is an estimate, so check how your sales figure is calculated.
Recommendation: Look at which channels and categories have the most returns, and fix the cause, such as sizing or product descriptions.
